/E (The coral reefs of Kota Kinabalu face mounting pressures from coastal development, sedimentation, and climate-induced thermal stress, underscored by the 2024 global bleaching event. While effective monitoring is critical for safeguarding marine biodiversity, traditional surveys often miss fine-scale structural details, and advanced scanning technologies remain costly. This project explores the potential of underwater 3D photogrammetry using consumer-grade action cameras as an accessible, non-invasive method for assessing reef condition and recovery. By capturing overlapping imagery across 10 survey sites in Kota Kinabalu, the workflow reconstructs high-resolution 3D models and orthomosaics. These digital assets allow researchers to accurately quantify live coral cover, analyze community composition, and examine post-bleaching taxonomic responses without disturbing fragile habitats. Integrated with machine-learning-assisted spatial annotation, the process streamlines analysis to detect patterns of bleaching, physical degradation, and early recruitment, creating an enduring digital baseline. By transforming accessible equipment into reliable analytical tools, this approach provides a practical framework for local monitoring teams, supporting proactive reef management and strengthening regional environmental resilience.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
