207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 124 0 R
/E (Universities across ASEAN are making public climate commitments, yet few can demonstrate whether their planned initiatives will actually deliver them. Universiti Malaya \(UM\) has pledged carbon neutrality by 2030 and net zero by 2050, but its institutional planning documents set targets without quantified reduction trajectories or scenario testing. This study addresses that gap by developing a scenario model of UM's operational greenhouse gas \(GHG\) emissions using the Low Emissions Analysis Platform \(LEAP\). Following the GHG Protocol, the model covers Scope 1 \(stationary combustion, mobile combustion and fugitive emissions\) and Scope 2 \(purchased electricity\). It establishes a baseline inventory, identifies emissions hotspots, and projects alternative decarbonisation pathways to 2050, ranging from business-as-usual to accelerated measures such as energy efficiency, on-campus solar and national grid decarbonisation. These pathways are then tested against UM's 2030 and 2050 targets to evaluate whether existing initiatives are sufficient and what additional action would be required. The work builds on the author's emissions data compilation and GHG calculations for UM's Carbon Neutrality Acceleration project. The study offers a transferable approach for regional universities to turn climate pledges into accountable, measurable pathways.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
