243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (Rapid urbanisation increasingly severs the bond between residents and the spaces they inhabit, eroding the sense of place that underpins environmental stewardship and community resilience. This project investigates how placemaking interventions grounded in artscape and ecological art principles can restore that connection within car-centric urban environments, in direct support of SDG 11 \(Sustainable Cities and Communities\) and SDG 15 \(Life on Land\). Drawing on established literature linking sense of place to stewardship behaviour, we worked on a site that is visually incoherent, ecologically sparse, and socially underutilised. As part of an actual upcoming urban intervention, we developed a suite of site-specific, monumental-scale design proposals to transform a passive slope into an active visual landmark legible to both motorists and pedestrians. In our proposal, integrated vegetation enhances local biodiversity and softens hard infrastructure, while heritage and cultural references strengthen institutional identity, together advancing SDG 4 \(Quality Education\) by embedding environmental literacy within the built environment itself. These live interventions aim to cultivate meaningful lived experiences, invite dwelling, and foster emotional attachment to place. Upon implementation, we anticipate measurable improvements in threshold legibility, greater pedestrian dwell time, and enriched ecological value along the corridor. Beyond the site, working on a real-world commission has fundamentally shaped how we approach problem solving. We have learnt how to navigate the complexity of balancing aesthetic ambition with ecological responsibility and genuine community need. We hope our work models how youth, empowered through authentic project experiences, can become active contributors to the broader sustainable urban future in Asian cities.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
